I.A Brand Identity Strategy
Brand identity strategy generally works in layers, built in a specific order — each layer depends on the one before it. Here's the structure, using Aninsane as the live example:

+++

Layer 1: Positioning (the strategic core)
This answers: who are you, for whom, and why does it matter to them? It's not visual or even verbal yet — it's a decision. For you, this was: premium painterly fantasy portraits with atmospheric lighting, for people who want their character to feel like it belongs in a magical story. This layer is the hardest to get right and the most important, because everything else just expresses this decision. Get this wrong, and a beautiful logo won't save you.

Positioning Statement: Aninsane creates premium character art in a modern gacha-splash-art aesthetic (Genshin/HSR/Arknights-inspired) for OC owners and VTubers who want their character to feel real — starting with illustration (portraits, headshots, full body), with character design sheets and VTuber-ready model art as growing specialties.

Proof points to build toward (things a stranger could look at and believe the claim):
Portfolio pieces that show range within fantasy — different species/aesthetics — while keeping lighting/mood consistent, proving the mood is the throughline, not luck
Before/after or process snippets showing how a flat sketch becomes atmospheric (this also plays to your ability to explain your craft)
Testimonials/reactions that specifically mention feeling — "this is literally how I imagined them" is a proof point for the identity-validation angle

One practical implication: this audience combo tells you where to actually post and find clients — OC/Toyhouse and fantasy-OC Twitter/Bluesky communities, and VTuber commission marketplaces (VGen, Twitter VTuber-artist tags) rather than generalist commission platforms like Fiverr, where premium/painterly work tends to get undervalued.

+++

Layer 2: Differentiators (the "why me, not them")
These are the specific, ownable pieces stacked together that make the positioning defensible — not just "I'm good," but "I'm this specific combination." For you: rendering/lighting skill + fantasy setting + solo portrait format + premium tier. Individually common, together rare.

Here's the honest test: if I described your current positioning to another gacha-style artist and asked "does this describe you too?" — a lot of skilled artists in this space would say yes. "Gacha-splash-art aesthetic, atmospheric lighting, for OC owners and VTubers" describes the genre and audience you're choosing to work in — it doesn't yet describe something only you do within that genre. Naming a crowded aesthetic and a common audience isn't ownership of either.

Why this happened: we built the stack in the right order (style → subject → niche → tier), but every piece we picked was still a category, not a personal signature. "Fantasy," "gacha aesthetic," "solo portraits," "premium" — these are all things thousands of artists could also claim. Real differentiation usually comes from something narrower and more personal than a genre choice. A few honest categories of what that could actually be:

1. A visual fingerprint — something a viewer starts to recognize as specifically yours even without seeing your signature. Top commission artists in saturated styles almost always have this: a particular way they render eyes, a signature color grading choice, a recurring compositional habit, a specific way light always hits skin in their work. Right now, does anything in your art repeat across pieces in a way people could point to and say "that's an Aninsane thing"? If you're not sure, that's honest information — it likely doesn't exist yet as a deliberate choice.

2. A narrower thematic lane, not just "gacha style" — this connects back to the regal/gothic/fae/monstrous question I asked earlier that got sidelined by the service-format discussion. "I draw in gacha style" is a genre. "I draw gacha-style characters who always feel regal and slightly melancholic" is a lane. The narrower and weirder the lane, the more ownable it is — ironically, going more specific here is what makes you stand out in a crowded market, not less.

3. A personality/process differentiator, independent of the art itself — this is actually where you may already have a real, underused edge. Earlier in this conversation, you showed genuine depth in why lighting works — subsurface scattering, rim light, warm/cool logic — not just "I copy the look." Most artists in this style can execute it without being able to explain it. If you make that visible (process breakdowns, explaining your lighting choices, teaching-adjacent content), that becomes a memorable trait about you as a creator, separate from whether your art style itself is unique yet.

