My thematic lane: "Divine/mythical mark carrying moral weight". Human or human-adjacent characters marked by divine or mythical origin — dragon-blood, angelic/demonic nature, forest-goddess purity — where that mark carries real narrative weight (blessing, curse, burden, sacrifice), rendered with soft ambient lighting in an atmospheric environment that reflects the character's story.

Your actual differentiator, stated precisely: solo character, shown closely/intimately (not a wide establishing shot), but placed within an environment that carries mood and story — with the intensity of that environment scaling to match the emotional message of the piece — all rendered through soft, ambient light rather than harsh direct highlights, even in dramatic moments.

+++

Layer 3: Personality/voice (how it feels to interact with)
This is tone — how you caption posts, talk to clients, present yourself. It should feel consistent with the positioning. A "magical, atmospheric portrait" brand probably doesn't caption things in blunt internet-slang meme humor — it'd undercut the mood you're selling. We haven't locked this for you yet.

Layer 4: Visual identity (logo, colors, fonts, templates)
This is the layer most people think "branding" means — but notice it's last. A logo is just a symbol for everything above it. This is why we correctly held off on logo concepts earlier; designing this first would've meant guessing at what it's supposed to represent.

Layer 5: Touchpoints (where it shows up consistently)
Portfolio layout, Instagram bio, commission sheet, watermark, pinned post — every place a client encounters you. Consistency here is what turns "I saw pretty art once" into "I recognize that artist."

The critical rule connecting all of it: each layer should be traceable back to Layer 1. If a logo color, a caption style, or a portfolio choice doesn't obviously connect back to "premium painterly fantasy portraits, atmospheric and magical," it's noise diluting the brand rather than building it.
